{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Implemented — change 1491 (https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1491)\n\nAll six specification points are implemented. Per lane:\n\n**1. Gerrit verify — \"if you touch it, you own it.\"** `_build-and-test.yml`'s\npip-audit job no longer runs an unconditional shell loop; it calls\n`scripts/dependency_audit.py gate --lane \"$LANE\"`, where LANE resolves to\n`gerrit` iff `inputs.gerrit-refspec != ''` (the reusable's only lane\ndiscriminator) and `branch` otherwise. On the gerrit lane a CRITICAL/HIGH\nadvisory blocks ONLY when the change touches the dependency map.\n\n**The \"touches the dependency map\" signal**, as implemented: the changed-file\nset of the patchset (`git diff --name-only HEAD^..HEAD`) is matched against\n`uv.lock`, `pyproject.toml` (anywhere in the tree), `poetry.lock`, and\n`requirements*.txt` / `constraints*.txt`. Deliberately the dependency MAP, not\na per-package comparison against the advisory's own package — a finer match\nwould let a dependency-map change land while the closure it produced was still\nvulnerable. Two follow-on decisions worth stating explicitly:\n* the Gerrit lane deepens the checkout by one (`git fetch --deepen=1`) before\n  diffing, because a shallow patchset checkout has no parent object;\n* if the diff is STILL unresolvable the gate FAILS CLOSED (treats the change as\n  map-touching). An unknown diff must never be the reason an advisory goes\n  unblocked.\n\n**2/6. Red scheduled main blocks RELEASES only.** `release.yml`'s `authorize`\njob gained a `--lane release` gate. `authorize` is the job every publishing job\nreaches through `needs:`, so the gate cannot be routed around — and that is\nasserted by a test that walks the `needs:` graph, not by inspection.\n\n**3. Escalation + dedup.** New daily `dependency-advisory-canary.yml`, modelled\non `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` (same ticket-store plumbing, signing-key\nmaterialization, flush-unpushed guard). It files ONE bug tagged\n`dependency-advisory-alert`, comments at most once per 24h while the advisory\nstays outstanding, and closes the ticket when it clears.\n\nThe canary's dedup was **NOT reusable as-is** — `_find_alert_ticket` and\n`_recent_marker_comment` were module-private and the latter hard-coded\n`BRIDGE_CANARY_ALERT:`, so two lanes sharing them would have collapsed onto one\nmarker and muted each other's accumulation caps. As the spec directs, it was\nEXTRACTED rather than duplicated: `scripts/alert_dedup.py` now holds\n`find_alert_ticket` + `recent_marker_comment(…, marker, …)`, and\n`canary_bridge.py` delegates to it (its existing oracle suite still passes\nunchanged, and a new test asserts no second copy was left behind).\n\n**4. Severity bar.** CRITICAL/HIGH fail, MEDIUM warns, LOW is tracked. Verified\nagainst the installed pip-audit 2.10.1: its `VulnerabilityResult` is\n`(id, description, fix_versions, aliases, published)` — it carries **no\nseverity at all**. Severity is therefore enriched from OSV\n(`database_specific.severity`) through the injected runner, and the documented\nfallback applies: **an advisory with no severity is treated as HIGH and fails.**\nEnrichment is fail-soft, which means an OSV outage makes the gate STRICTER,\nnever weaker. Documented in the runbook's severity table.\n\n**5. override-dependencies as a documented escape hatch.**\n`docs/dependency-advisory-runbook.md` §4 gives the order — (a) upgrade the\ndirect dependency, (b) if an upstream cap blocks that, `override-dependencies`\nwith a justification naming the advisory, the capping package, and why the\ncombination is believed safe, (c) track the upstream fix — with a copy-pasteable\n`[tool.uv]` block carrying a REMOVE-WHEN line. The banked uv knowledge survives\nverbatim and is pinned by test: `constraint-dependencies` can only NARROW and\nreturns UNSATISFIABLE as its EXPECTED output; `override-dependencies` REPLACES\nthe requirement; `uv lock --upgrade-package` is a NO-OP against an upstream cap;\noverrides are a workspace directive not published in wheel metadata. §5 states\nthe verification set (`uv lock --check`, `uv sync --locked --extra dev`,\n`pip-audit` over the whole environment to confirm the old pin masked nothing).\n\n## Proven by test, not by inspection\n\n`tests/scripts/test_dependency_audit.py` (73 cases) and\n`tests/unit/test_dependency_advisory_wiring.py` (24 cases), plus\n`tests/scripts/test_alert_dedup.py` (11). The three the spec demanded:\n* `test_gerrit_lane_blocks_when_dependency_map_touched` /\n  `test_gate_gerrit_exit_one_when_lock_is_touched` — blocked, exit 1;\n* `test_gerrit_lane_does_not_block_when_map_untouched` /\n  `test_gate_gerrit_exit_zero_for_an_unrelated_change` — NOT blocked, exit 0,\n  and the finding is still printed (ownership, not suppression);\n* `test_release_lane_blocks` / `test_gate_release_exit_one_on_an_outstanding_advisory`.\n\nThe wiring suite parses the workflow YAML so a correct module cannot sit behind\na gate that never runs — the concern raised by the sibling finding on `599e`. It\nasserts the lane expression, that the old unconditional shell gate is GONE, the\ndeepen step exists, the release gate is in `authorize`, every publishing job\ndescends from it, the canary is scheduled and its escalation step survives a\nblocking verdict (`continue-on-error` on the audit step — the ticket IS the\nescalation), and that the runbook still carries each banked uv fact.\n\nLocal: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `check_raw_git_writes`, `check_docs_index`,\n`check_comment_hygiene` all clean; `pytest tests/unit tests/scripts` 3139 passed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786101546920705001,"uuid":"19730d49-8999-4cd4-9c1d-8cb89f00bcb7"}